WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.010 position:63%
It's finally happening.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:06.410 position:63%
After an over 13-year
wait and so many memes,

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.450 position:63%
Grand Theft Auto VI is finally
coming out this 19th of November.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.500 position:63%
Please don't change the date, Rockstar.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.060 position:63%
I cannot refilm this. [laughs]

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.210 position:63%
But the point is, with
GTA VI finally here,

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:18.810 position:63%
can we predict

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.550 position:63%
how long until GTA VII?

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.900 position:63%
Yes. We're gonna run the numbers,
do some mathematics, extrapolate,

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.260 position:63%
and work out when GTA VII will be with us.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.090 position:63%
And I am driving down
the street right now.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.190 position:63%
You can't tell, but my hair

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.530 position:63%
is being swept back in the wind.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:34.660 position:63%
Let's do it.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:40.420 position:63%
[upbeat music]

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.040 position:63%
So let's get our data sorted.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.300 position:63%
The very first Grand Theft Auto came out
in 1997 and started a very neat two-year

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:49.730 position:63%
cycle. Two years later, we got GTA II.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.050 position:63%
Two years after that, GTA III.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:57.190 position:63%
Then one year to Vice City, but that was
kind of a spin-off, but not, of GTA III.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.520 position:63%
We'll leave that in the data set for now.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:03.979 position:63%
And then two years until San Andreas,
and that was the end of the neat bit.

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:06.250 position:63%
And I'm including all of
them as main releases.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.310 position:63%
I'm ignoring re-releases and
different consoles and all that jazz.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.470 position:63%
Main sequence only.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:15.050 position:63%
Although it does now mean when

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:18.740 position:63%
GTA IV came out, it was actually
the sixth one in the installation.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:19.910 position:63%
That took a bit longer.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.310 position:63%
That was three and a half years.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:26.700 position:63%
And now it starts to spiral,
5.4 years until GTA V,

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.690 position:63%
and we're expecting now 13.2 years.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.110 position:63%
But we can still put those

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:32.600 position:63%
on a plot.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:33.940 position:63%
Here they are.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:35.630 position:63%
We draw a straight line through them,

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:37.210 position:63%
and we predict

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:43.130 position:63%
where that line for GTA VII
hits the vertical year axis,

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.250 position:63%
and it's the 4th of October in 2022,

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:52.070 position:63%
the past. Because there
was such a big wait until

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:57.430 position:63%
GTA VI, averaging the data
out, we need one from the past.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:01.290 position:63%
So the official extrapolation is GTA VII

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:05.560 position:63%
will have had come out in the year 2022.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:11.880 position:63%
[upbeat music]

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.310 position:63%
Now, that prediction is obviously
wrong because it's negative,

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.430 position:63%
and predicting things in
the future, extrapolating,

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:20.870 position:63%
it's art as well as science.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.280 position:63%
You gotta look at your prediction and say,

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.530 position:63%
"Does that actually make sense?" But
it's not just art, it is also science,

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:29.880 position:63%
and you extrapolation fans will
have seen the R squared value,

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.560 position:63%
which in that case is 0.822, and that's
kind of a measure of how close the

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.790 position:63%
line is to the data points, 'cause
it's not gonna go through all of them.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.880 position:63%
We want it to be as close as possible.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:45.980 position:63%
And if that R squared value was one, that
would be bang on through all the points.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:49.790 position:63%
And 0.822 might sound close,
but it's actually terrible.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:53.650 position:63%
If we were to upgrade to a
quadratic, ooh, that's fancy,

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.610 position:63%
now our R squared value is 0.955.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.860 position:63%
Way closer. And we get a new prediction.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.430 position:63%
It says we're gonna get GTA VI
on the 1st of May in the year

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:05.880 position:63%
2033.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.890 position:63%
But we are also being
led astray, potentially.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:12.790 position:63%
I mentioned this in passing
in my Count Binface video,

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:17.200 position:63%
where I was trying to extrapolate data,
and I said how a quadratic always fits

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.970 position:63%
better than a linear just because there's
more parts to a quadratic in the equation.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:26.530 position:63%
You've got more levers and dials to
move around to match it, and in fact,

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:31.900 position:63%
the higher the order of your polynomial,
the better it will match just by default.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:33.590 position:63%
Doesn't mean it's getting any better.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.870 position:63%
In fact, if we increase the
order of our polynomial,

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:41.450 position:63%
it gets closer and closer and closer
until order seven, perfect match.

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:43.700 position:63%
Like, through every single data point.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:45.600 position:63%
And you're like, "Well,
that's gotta be the one."

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:48.710 position:63%
That predicts the 14th of
April in the year 2068.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:49.620 position:63%
But here's the thing.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:51.630 position:63%
We have eight data points.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:57.840 position:63%
If you have n data points, you can always
find a polynomial of order n minus one,

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:02.320 position:63%
so one less, that goes exactly through
every data point with an R squared value

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:04.270 position:63%
of one, but it's meaningless.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:07.430 position:63%
I can actually pick any future date

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.510 position:63%
and use an order eight and hit it exactly.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:16.530 position:63%
So this equation here, that predicts
that GTA VII will come out on Pi Day

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:17.500 position:63%
next year.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:19.250 position:63%
It's not going to.

00:04:20.279 --> 00:04:22.010 position:63%
There always exists

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:26.230 position:63%
a polynomial which will predict
any future data point you want,

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:28.770 position:63%
as long as you're prepared to
make it as ridiculous as you want.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:31.160 position:63%
So this is not helping.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:41.000 position:63%
[upbeat music]

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:41.520 position:63%
[slapping]

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:42.470 position:63%
Okay, new plan.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:46.670 position:63%
We're gonna take the two-year original
release cycle as the baseline,

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:51.000 position:63%
and that ran until San Andreas,
which came out 728 days,

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.330 position:63%
so basically exactly two
years after the previous one.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:57.470 position:63%
And if we look at the
increasing gaps after that,

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:01.480 position:63%
we can try and extrapolate the gap size.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:07.170 position:63%
And if we use these values, we predict
that the gap from GTA VI to VII will be

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:12.790 position:63%
5,430 days, which puts it on
the 1st of October in the year

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:14.230 position:63%
2041.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:16.230 position:63%
Is that plausible?

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:17.660 position:63%
I mean, [laughs]

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.230 position:63%
I wouldn't be that surprised if
GTA VII came out in the 2040s.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:25.990 position:63%
And this method's fine.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:28.300 position:63%
Again, we can play the same game.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:29.340 position:63%
We can keep chasing it.

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:30.760 position:63%
We can switch to a quadratic.

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:34.200 position:63%
We can go, "Oh, what if we make this
exponential?" We can try and fit loads of

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:35.040 position:63%
things to this.

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:36.440 position:63%
I don't know.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.480 position:63%
At this point, I feel like we're
just looking at the data and we're

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:43.690 position:63%
over-fitting. We're just, like, looking at

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:46.600 position:63%
mathematical tea leaves trying
to guess what's in the future.

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:47.530 position:63%
I feel like

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:50.730 position:63%
we need to bring more logic to this party.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:54.900 position:63%
Hey, quickly, it's me, Matt,
from a future video about darts.

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:58.960 position:63%
I'm just here to say if you're a Patreon
supporter who gets a physical Christmas

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.670 position:63%
card, the deadline is this
Friday, the 20th of November.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:04.410 position:63%
After that, you'll still get a card,

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:06.270 position:63%
I just can't guarantee
it'll be by Christmas,

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:08.100 position:63%
and it's gonna be your
own unique dartboard

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:10.010 position:63%
with whatever's going on over here.

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:11.580 position:63%
Ah, I'm gonna personalise them.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:12.470 position:63%
I'm very excited.

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:14.030 position:63%
Make sure you sign up in time.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:16.690 position:63%
Oh, digital cards, don't worry about that.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:18.130 position:63%
You'll get those easily.

00:06:18.936 --> 00:06:22.576 position:63%
Okay, so what do we do if the
raw numbers aren't helping us?

00:06:22.616 --> 00:06:25.696 position:63%
Well, you wanna use
some mathematical logic.

00:06:25.736 --> 00:06:29.856 position:63%
See if there's a reason why one
prediction is better than the others.

00:06:29.896 --> 00:06:32.796 position:63%
And I thought I would subtract
the two-year baseline,

00:06:32.836 --> 00:06:37.256 position:63%
so I'm just looking at, like, the
delay beyond the two-year cycle,

00:06:37.296 --> 00:06:41.106 position:63%
which does give us one fewer data
point, 'cause one of them goes to zero.

00:06:41.476 --> 00:06:42.276 position:63%
But if you take

00:06:42.836 --> 00:06:47.976 position:63%
the overhead that's been increasing,
and you apply an exponential fit,

00:06:48.016 --> 00:06:50.846 position:63%
so what are we multiplying it by
each time to get the next one?

00:06:51.836 --> 00:06:52.636 position:63%
It's e.

00:06:53.376 --> 00:06:54.356 position:63%
Isn't that ridiculous?

00:06:54.396 --> 00:06:55.196 position:63%
It's so close.

00:06:55.236 --> 00:06:57.446 position:63%
It's, like, I think 4% off e.

00:06:57.896 --> 00:07:01.346 position:63%
If GTA VII had come out on the 6th

00:07:02.036 --> 00:07:02.846 position:63%
of November,

00:07:03.476 --> 00:07:05.186 position:63%
exactly e. But it's

00:07:05.816 --> 00:07:08.726 position:63%
so close. If you're not familiar with e,

00:07:09.356 --> 00:07:13.096 position:63%
it's the unexpected number that
shows up when pi is not available.

00:07:13.136 --> 00:07:15.796 position:63%
I mean, a lot of these puzzles and, like,

00:07:15.836 --> 00:07:17.926 position:63%
predicting growth into the future is e,

00:07:18.556 --> 00:07:20.006 position:63%
so I feel like this makes sense.

00:07:20.316 --> 00:07:25.400 position:63%
And if you continue the e extrapolation,
GTA VII will come out on the

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:28.086 position:63%
29th of May, 2057.

00:07:28.876 --> 00:07:29.676 position:63%
So

00:07:30.396 --> 00:07:31.196 position:63%
there it is.

00:07:32.016 --> 00:07:35.206 position:63%
That's the official
Stand-up Maths prediction.

00:07:35.576 --> 00:07:36.256 position:63%
29th of May,

00:07:36.296 --> 00:07:37.126 position:63%
[laughs]

00:07:37.256 --> 00:07:38.526 position:63%
2057,

00:07:39.156 --> 00:07:42.756 position:63%
because I'm arguing the e
makes that one more likely.

00:07:42.796 --> 00:07:43.886 position:63%
Now, did I just

00:07:44.536 --> 00:07:48.256 position:63%
analyse loads and loads of ways you could
extrapolate the data and see if any of

00:07:48.296 --> 00:07:50.136 position:63%
them accidentally were near a maths thing?

00:07:50.176 --> 00:07:50.976 position:63%
Maybe.

00:07:51.736 --> 00:07:54.380 position:63%
Maybe some of the gaps
were weirdly close [laughs]

00:07:54.416 --> 00:07:56.726 position:63%
to a Fibonacci sequence,
and I nearly did that.

00:07:56.776 --> 00:07:58.456 position:63%
But no, I'm sticking with it.

00:07:58.496 --> 00:07:59.296 position:63%
That's it.

00:07:59.816 --> 00:08:04.246 position:63%
You can't argue against a fit
that good, so there you are.

00:08:04.556 --> 00:08:06.746 position:63%
That's how you race

00:08:07.496 --> 00:08:08.426 position:63%
into the future.

00:08:09.116 --> 00:08:11.836 position:63%
Extrapolate data. See you in 2057.

00:08:11.876 --> 00:08:17.076 position:63%
[upbeat music]

00:08:17.116 --> 00:08:17.636 position:63%
Well, that's it.

00:08:17.676 --> 00:08:18.496 position:63%
That's the video.

00:08:18.536 --> 00:08:20.276 position:63%
Thank you so much for watching.

00:08:20.316 --> 00:08:24.176 position:63%
And if you're new here, all my
maths videos are just like this one,

00:08:24.216 --> 00:08:26.736 position:63%
so if you wanna see more,
make sure you... Oh!

